  "title": "A law recognizes its space rather than creating it",
  "subject": "What a law's allowed states determine about a graph, a sphere or ends",
  "conclusion": "A law's allowed states carry a graph exactly when its knot matroid is graphic, and a sphere when it is also cographic: they determine a 3-connected graph exactly and a 2-connected one up to Whitney twists. Presence is decided by Tutte's excluded minors, not by how the law was written, so a randomly written local law can carry a sphere while almost every balanced code carries none; the mathematics is classical.",
